{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HMSbzc59a9AoBNU7RXtbKM","type":"tool_result","content":"# WI-967 — The act ledger records the registries each act copied (spec of record)\n\n**WI:** WI-967 · **Status:** queued · **Delivers:** SR-178 (anchor)\n\nThe preparatory row OI-115 routes (ruled 2026-11-01, option (a)). The row\nkeeps its Context and Done-when, the one home of what closes it; this spec\nholds the problem. Its scope critique settles the rest before the claim.\n\n## Problem\n\nWI-963 (the trunk mint drops amendment records a merged range already\nsettled) passed its checkpoint, and its gate raised one convergence-class\nMAJOR that dispute sitting 007-ADJUDICATE-1ae50f4 ruled FIX: a traced change\nrouted after a row's act settles silently when a later act in the range\nre-copies that registry for another row. The sound remedy needs to know\nwhich registries each act copied, and the act ledger\n(`docs/archive/last_approved/acts.toml`, IF-220) does not say. The snapshot\nwriter appends one entry per act with only `seq`, `date`, `approved` and\n`reattested`. Three act shapes copy a registry the entry never names (wi-963\nD-011, probed through the real producer):\n\n- a traced re-anchor (`intake.py snapshot --approves <registry>=<ref>` with\n  no ids) copies its registry and writes `approved = []`, `reattested = []`;\n- an act that mixes `--approves` on one registry with ids in another lists\n  only the second;\n- a re-seed copies every registry and lists only newly approved rows.\n\nComparing against the copy the re-attesting act's own commit wrote (the fix1\nbuild) does not close it either: every hand landing is a `git merge\n--squash`, whose one commit is the act's commit.\n\n## Scope\n\nEach act-ledger entry records the registries its act copied, for every act\nshape the snapshot writer produces: the entry's shape (IF-220), its one\nwriter, and its validators, with one RESYNC entry for adopters' ledgers.\nWI-963's settlement rule is **not** in scope; WI-963 rebases onto this\nrow's landing and builds it there.\n\nQuestions the scope critique answers before the claim:\n\n- how an entry written before this row is read (the ledger is\n  append-only and says \"Do not edit\");\n- every reader of the ledger, found by grep (`acceptance_record`,\n  `baseline_snapshot`, `gen_open_items` today), and whether the file's\n  authority over acts needs a `## Trust` section (rubric S2);\n- the landing order: it changes the act ledger the cycle runs on, so it\n  lands alone (revision 37).\n\n## The record (settled at the scope critique)\n\nEach new `[[act]]` entry gains one field, `copied`: the sorted list of the\nregistry files the act actually copied, each a repo-relative path from the\nsnapshot's own registry universe (the files `SNAPSHOT_TIERS` names, the\nneeds file included). The writer records the set it copied, never one\nreconstructed from row ids, so a traced re-anchor naming no row, a mixed\n`--approves` act and a re-seed each name every file they copied. An act\nthat copies nothing appends no entry, as today. `seq`, `date`, `approved`,\n`reattested` and `verdict` keep their meanings, and a copied file is no\nevidence that its rows were judged.\n\n**Legacy and new entries.** No existing entry is edited or backfilled. An\nentry without `copied` is a legacy entry: it parses and keeps its fields,\nand its copy set reads as unknown, never as empty, and is never inferred\nfrom ids, live files or snapshot history. The boundary is the commit, not\na marker: an entry a commit adds (its `seq` absent from the parent's\nledger, as `acceptance_record._added_acts` and `risk_acceptance_act`\nalready read it) must carry `copied`; and in a whole-file read, no entry\nmay omit it after one that carries it.\n\n## Architectural delta (within scope)\n\nThe producer (`baseline_snapshot`, CMP-006) and the IF-220 boundary are\nkept; IF-220's data domain gains `copied`, its notes the legacy reading,\nand its consumers list becomes the grep's. No new component, mode class\nor preparatory row.\n\n| Output | Depends on |\n|---|---|\n| new entry's `copied` | the files `copy_live` actually wrote in this act |\n| new entry's `approved`, `reattested`, `verdict` | unchanged: the act's named rows and verdict |\n| a reader's copy set for an entry | `copied` when present; unknown when absent |\n| validator verdict on an entry | whether the commit adds it (against the parent), and its place after the first entry carrying `copied` |\n\nCases: legacy entry (no `copied`), recorded entry, and the one transition\nthe writer makes at its first act after this row lands. WI-963 alone adds\nsettlement's consumption of `copied`.\n\n## Trust\n\nThe ledger has authority over acts: its entries back the acceptance\nrecord's act and verdict-backing rules.\n\n- **Producer.** `baseline_snapshot.copy_live` and `_record_act` alone write\n  `docs/archive/last_approved/acts.toml`, through `intake.py snapshot`,\n  run by a person or an authorized model session under the existing\n  approval rules. A refusal before copying is a failed call with no act.\n  An interrupted or timed-out call can leave unfinished copies or a\n  partial ledger; that is no completed act, and is repaired through the\n  supported act workflow before it is committed. This row adds no\n  transaction or recovery mechanism (PROCESS.md §3, \"When a guard is\n  owed\"; §6).\n- **Consumers (grep).** Direct readers of the file:\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/baseline_snapshot.py` (`_standing_acts`,\n  `acts_problems`, `_entry_problems`, `parse_acts`, `read_acts`,\n  `acts_findings`, `record_findings`, `_acts_at`, `_act_names`,\n  `risk_acceptance_act`, `risk_acceptance_view`),\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/acceptance_record.py` (`_ledger_acts`,\n  `_added_acts`, `reattested_between`, `lane_acted_set`,\n  `adjudication_backing_refusal`, `_wrote_ledger`, `SNAPSHOT_OWN_FILES`)\n  and `project-trajectory/scripts/gen_open_items.py` (`_ledger_entries`,\n  `verdict_reattest_block`). Entry-field consumers:\n  `project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/sitting.py` (`_named_line`,\n  `unaccepted_refusal`, `uncovered_refusal`, `_ACT_KINDS`; some callers\n  pass synthesized act-shaped dictionaries, which need no `copied`).\n  Findings consumer: `project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py` (through\n  `record_findings`). Producer route: `project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py`\n  (`_cmd_snapshot`).\n- **Ruling (one, shared).**\n  - *No ledger:* no entries and no copy evidence; the first successful\n    signing creates the file; no past act is fabricated from absence.\n  - *A le
